## Statement

Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $A_{\mathbb Z}$ be a finite free unital integer algebra with $A_{\mathbb Q}=\mathbb Q\otimes A_{\mathbb Z}$, and let $K$ be any characteristic-zero field. Suppose a finite-dimensional graded cyclic $A_K$-module $S_K$, with specified homogeneous generator $z_K$, is obtained from a homogeneous integer cyclic presentation $S_{\mathbb Z}=A_{\mathbb Z}/I_{\mathbb Z}$ by scalar extension, carrying $1+I_{\mathbb Z}$ to $z_K$. Suppose $S_K$ has a nonzero simple head $D_K$, with its specified grading normalization. Then the pair consisting of $D_K$ and the line spanned by the image $v$ of $z_K$ descends to $\mathbb Q$. More precisely there is a graded quotient $D_{\mathbb Q}$ of $S_{\mathbb Q}$ and $v_{\mathbb Q}\ne0$ such that $K\otimes D_{\mathbb Q}\cong D_K$ carries $1\otimes v_{\mathbb Q}$ to $v$. Consequently $A_{\mathbb Z}v$ is free of rank $\dim_KD_K$. If $D_K$ is absolutely simple, every nonzero quotient map from the specified $S_K$ to the fixed normalized $D_K$ has generator image on this same line, up to $K^\times$.

## Facts & Assumptions

[F1] [[thm-jacobson-radical-is-nilpotent-and-the-quotient-is-semisimple]] gives a nilpotent radical and semisimple quotient for every finite-dimensional algebra.

[F2] [[thm-wedderburn-artin-theorem]] and [[thm-simple-modules-over-semisimple-rings]] describe semisimple finite-dimensional algebras and their simples.

[F3] [[thm-schurs-lemma-for-modules]] applies to simple modules. Absolute simplicity forces scalar endomorphisms, by extending any commuting matrix to an algebraic closure and using an eigenvalue.

[A1] The Axiom of Choice is used only to obtain an algebraic closure of an arbitrary characteristic-zero field through [[thm-existence-of-algebraic-closures]]; it is authorized by [[def-axiom-of-choice]].

[A2] The integer presentation is an explicit prerequisite, not asserted here for the existing Hecke Specht construction. Its identification must preserve the specified generator.

## Proof

**Given:** The hypotheses in the statement, the Axiom of Choice, and F1–F3.

1.1 We first prove radical base change in characteristic zero without a splitting assumption on $K$. For a finite-dimensional algebra $B$ over an algebraically closed characteristic-zero field $L$, let $\beta_B(a,b)=\operatorname{Tr}(L_{ab}:B\to B)$. Every radical element is orthogonal to $B$, since its product with any element is in the nilpotent radical and its left multiplication has zero trace. Conversely take a composition series of the left regular $B$-module. By F1–F2, $B/J(B)$ is a product of matrix algebras $M_{n_i}(L)$: a finite-dimensional division algebra over $L$ equals $L$, since left multiplication by any of its elements has an eigenvalue, and a nonzero element minus that eigenvalue cannot be a nonunit. Trace is additive along an invariant filtration. Therefore the regular trace pairing is, on the semisimple quotient, $\sum_i m_i\operatorname{tr}(X_iY_i)$, where $m_i$ is the positive integer multiplicity of its column simple in the regular composition series. Every factor has positive multiplicity because the regular quotient contains it. This pairing is nondegenerate by testing matrix units, since all $m_i$ are nonzero in characteristic zero. Thus $\ker\beta_B=J(B)$. [F1, F2, algebra]

1.2 For any characteristic-zero field $F$ and finite-dimensional $F$-algebra $B$, use A1 to take an algebraic closure $L$. The matrix of $\beta_{L\otimes B}$ is the scalar extension of that of $\beta_B$. Kernel computation by row reduction therefore gives $\ker\beta_{L\otimes B}=L\otimes\ker\beta_B$. By step 1.1 this is the nilpotent ideal $J(L\otimes B)$. Faithful scalar extension implies that $\ker\beta_B$ is itself a nilpotent ideal. A nilpotent two-sided ideal kills each simple module: its image on a simple is either zero or the entire simple; the latter contradicts nilpotence. It is consequently contained in $J(B)$ by the intersection-of-maximal-left-ideals definition. The reverse inclusion follows from the zero-trace argument of step 1.1. Hence $J(B)=\ker\beta_B$. Apply this equality over $F=\mathbb Q$ and $F=K$ to the same regular trace matrix to obtain $J(A_K)=K\otimes J(A_{\mathbb Q})$. [F1, A1, step 1.1, algebra]

2.1 Put $S_{\mathbb Q}=\mathbb Q\otimes S_{\mathbb Z}$ and $D_{\mathbb Q}=S_{\mathbb Q}/J(A_{\mathbb Q})S_{\mathbb Q}$. Tensoring quotient presentations is right exact and $K/\mathbb Q$ is flat, so $K\otimes D_{\mathbb Q}=S_K/J(A_K)S_K$. This last quotient is precisely the semisimple head: $A_K/J(A_K)$ is semisimple, so the quotient is semisimple, while every simple quotient kills $J(A_K)S_K$. Equivalently, intersections of maximal submodules equal $J(A_K)S_K$, since the intersection of the maximal submodules of a finite semisimple module is zero. The assumed head is simple, so this quotient is $D_K$. Its rational precursor is nonzero and simple: a proper nonzero rational submodule would remain proper and nonzero after faithful scalar extension. [F1, F2, step 1.2, algebra]

2.2 The radical of a finite-dimensional $\mathbb Q$-graded algebra is homogeneous. Indeed all scaling automorphisms $a_d\mapsto t^d a_d$ preserve its characteristic ideal $J$, for $t\in\mathbb Q^\times$. If $a=\sum_{d\in E}a_d\in J$ with finite degree set $E$, take $t=2^j$ for $j=0,\ldots,|E|-1$. The resulting coefficient matrix $((2^d)^j)_{j,d}$ is Vandermonde in the pairwise distinct rational numbers $2^d$, hence invertible; each $a_d$ is a rational linear combination of elements of $J$. Thus $J(A_{\mathbb Q})S_{\mathbb Q}$ is homogeneous and $D_{\mathbb Q}$ has the required quotient grading. Put $v_{\mathbb Q}=z_{\mathbb Q}+J(A_{\mathbb Q})S_{\mathbb Q}$. It is nonzero: otherwise cyclicity would force $D_{\mathbb Q}=0$. The scalar-extension quotient isomorphism preserves the generator, and hence its image is exactly $v$, including its degree. Any prescribed normalization shift is imposed simultaneously on both modules. [step 2.1, algebra]

3.1 The orbit $A_{\mathbb Z}v_{\mathbb Q}$ is generated as an abelian group by the images of a finite integer basis of $A_{\mathbb Z}$. It is torsion-free because it is a subgroup of a rational vector space. Its rational span is $D_{\mathbb Q}$, by cyclicity, so its rank equals $\dim_{\mathbb Q}D_{\mathbb Q}=\dim_KD_K$. The generator-preserving scalar-extension isomorphism identifies this orbit with $A_{\mathbb Z}v$. A finitely generated torsion-free abelian group is free, which proves the lattice assertion. [step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]

3.2 If a fixed normalized $D_K$ is absolutely simple, a nonzero map $S_K\to D_K$ is surjective and kills the radical. Since the head is already the simple $D_K$, such maps factor through its canonical quotient and differ by an invertible endomorphism of $D_K$. This endomorphism is scalar: over an algebraic closure it has an eigenvalue; its difference from that scalar has a kernel, and simplicity forces the difference to vanish; the matrix's original entries put the scalar in $K$. Thus every allowed generator image is a scalar multiple of the constructed image, and scaling the lattice preserves freeness and rank. No claim concerning an arbitrary vector in $D_K$ was used. [F3, A1, step 2.1, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
